many things that

00:01:34.860 --> 00:01:37.390
happened during the holidays.

00:01:37.390 --> 00:01:41.640
But we will spend our time,
the future weeks basically,

00:01:41.640 --> 00:01:43.840
going over all the cool
things that happened.

00:01:43.840 --> 00:01:44.010
MARK MANDEL: I don't understand.

00:01:44.010 --> 00:01:46.350
You figure that people shut
down over that period, but no.

00:01:46.350 --> 00:01:47.470
Apparently, we were
just like let's really

00:01:47.470 --> 00:01:48.390
sell a whole bunch of stuff.

00:01:48.390 --> 00:01:49.806
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
They work harder.

00:01:49.806 --> 00:01:51.310
It's like, please,
people, chill.

00:01:51.310 --> 00:01:55.830
Anyway, the first
one is this blog post

00:01:55.830 --> 00:01:58.470
that was that is part of the
Google Cloud Platform blog

00:01:58.470 --> 00:02:01.980
post on how we secure
our infrastructure.

00:02:01.980 --> 00:02:04.800
As in, how we secure our
own Google Cloud Platform

00:02:04.800 --> 00:02:05.520
infrastructure.

00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:08.270
So the blog post is a
pretty short blog post

00:02:08.270 --> 00:02:11.130
but it actually points
to a security zine

00:02:11.130 --> 00:02:14.970
white paper that is
definitely worth reading.

00:02:14.970 --> 00:02:17.820
It has-- I really like that
it starts with a CIO level

00:02:17.820 --> 00:02:18.350
summary.

00:02:18.350 --> 00:02:21.520
So there's basically the four
points that you care about,

00:02:21.520 --> 00:02:23.600
what they are going
to be talking about.

00:02:23.600 --> 00:02:26.220
But you can go into
it in more detail,

00:02:26.220 --> 00:02:31.830
and see how we protect you
against, like, DDoS attacks,

00:02:31.830 --> 00:02:35.590
or how encryption arrest
works, and how deletion of data

00:02:35.590 --> 00:02:36.090
happens.

00:02:36.090 --> 00:02:37.640
This is like so much stuff.

00:02:37.640 --> 00:02:39.590
There's all of those
security layers,

00:02:39.590 --> 00:02:42.880
and it goes in to detail on
every single one of them.

00:02:42.880 --> 00:02:45.220
So it's definitely worth--

00:02:45.220 --> 00:02:46.365
as a curiosity, really.

00:02:46.365 --> 00:02:48.400
But if you are
interested in security,

00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:50.280
it's definitely a
good paper to read.

00:02:50.280 --> 00:02:52.321
MARK MANDEL: Actually,
it's worth noting, really,

00:02:52.321 --> 00:02:55.020
this is like the beginning of a
series of stuff around security

00:02:55.020 --> 00:02:56.790
and identity that are going
to be happening on the blog.

00:02:56.790 --> 00:02:58.787
So definitely stay tuned
if that's your thing

00:02:58.787 --> 00:03:01.120
and you're really concerned
about security and identity.

00:03:01.120 --> 00:03:02.100
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:03:02.100 --> 00:03:06.770
And if you're not concerned
about security, you should.

00:03:06.770 --> 00:03:09.960
No, but it's a very interesting
white paper nonetheless.

00:03:09.960 --> 00:03:11.250
MARK MANDEL: Absolutely.

00:03:11.250 --> 00:03:12.660
So the next one is--

00:03:12.660 --> 00:03:15.450
I don't know why, this
just makes me really happy.

00:03:15.450 --> 00:03:17.616
I know a lot of people have
wanted this for a while.

00:03:17.616 --> 00:03:20.340
We now have actual icons and
simple architectural diagrams

00:03:20.340 --> 00:03:21.600
for designing stuff.

00:03:21.600 --> 00:03:23.070
Like, when you're building
out your architecture

00:03:23.070 --> 00:03:24.445
on Google Cloud
Platform, and you

00:03:24.445 --> 00:03:26.070
want to design it
out using either,

00:03:26.070 --> 00:03:28.830
say, Google Slides, or
Lucidchart, or PowerPoint,

00:03:28.830 --> 00:03:31.380
or if you just need
PNGs or SVGs to put

00:03:31.380 --> 00:03:33.150
inside your favorite
diagramming software.

00:03:33.150 --> 00:03:33.480
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:03:33.480 --> 00:03:35.479
MARK MANDEL: We have those
now available to use!

00:03:35.479 --> 00:03:38.049
So you can use them, which I
think is actually really handy.

00:03:38.049 --> 00:03:40.590
FRANCESC CAMPOY: I'm incredibly
excited about it, because I--

00:03:40.590 --> 00:03:44.250
like, it is sad to say, but
very often I would Google--

00:03:44.250 --> 00:03:46.634
I would Search on Google
for our own products

00:03:46.634 --> 00:03:47.550
to find that I could--

00:03:47.550 --> 00:03:48.425
MARK MANDEL: Exactly.

00:03:48.425 --> 00:03:50.550
FRANCESC CAMPOY: --you
know, our own presentations.

00:03:50.550 --> 00:03:52.660
I'm very happy that we
finally got a repository.

00:03:52.660 --> 00:03:56.020
And it is open, so
anyone can use it.

00:03:56.020 --> 00:03:57.660
So if you're thinking
about talking

00:03:57.660 --> 00:04:00.980
about how you use Google Cloud
Platform on a presentation,

00:04:00.980 --> 00:04:02.640
feel free to go and use them.

00:04:02.640 --> 00:04:03.640
MARK MANDEL: Definitely.

00:04:03.640 --> 00:04:04.770
Now, we have one more.

00:04:04.770 --> 00:04:07.061
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah, one
more cool thing of the week.

00:04:07.061 --> 00:04:10.970
And this one is really cool to
start with because, of course,

00:04:10.970 --> 00:04:11.940
it's written in Go.

00:04:11.940 --> 00:04:15.720
But also because it
is about something

00:04:15.720 --> 00:04:18.660
that many people have
asked me about how

00:04:18.660 --> 00:04:22.079
to do it, which is, how do
you do continuous deployment

00:04:22.079 --> 00:04:24.180
to Google Cloud Platform?

00:04:24.180 --> 00:04:26.760
Specifically, how do you
do continuous deployment

00:04:26.760 --> 00:04:30.570
to App Engine and
Container Engine?

00:04:30.570 --> 00:04:32.970
And turns out the
New York Times,

00:04:32.970 --> 00:04:35.880
they have been using Google
Platform for quite a while.

00:04:35.880 --> 00:04:37.860
They had this issue,
and they solved it.

00:04:37.860 --> 00:04:39.701
And they open
sourced the project.

00:04:39.701 --> 00:04:41.200
MARK MANDEL: Yeah,
it's pretty cool.

00:04:41.200 --> 00:04:43.946
There's a New-- sorry,
there's an open source

00:04:43.946 --> 00:04:46.320
continuous integration and
continuous deployment platform

00:04:46.320 --> 00:04:48.960
called Drone that's
written in Go.

00:04:48.960 --> 00:04:52.560
And they built plugins
for GKE and GAE,

00:04:52.560 --> 00:04:55.320
so you can do continuous
delivery and deployment

00:04:55.320 --> 00:04:56.640
to those two platforms.

00:04:56.640 --> 00:04:57.000
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:04:57.000 --> 00:04:57.520
MARK MANDEL: It's pretty cool.

00:04:57.520 --> 00:04:58.420
It's all built
around Containers.

00:04:58.420 --> 00:04:59.160
Yay Containers.

00:04:59.160 --> 00:05:01.230
We all love Containers.

00:05:01.230 --> 00:05:02.160
And it's pretty neat.

00:05:02.160 --> 00:05:05.040
So, super happy to see the
community and the ecosystem

00:05:05.040 --> 00:05:07.240
around Google Cloud
Platform continue to grow.

00:05:07.240 --> 00:05:08.156
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:05:08.156 --> 00:05:09.590
It is definitely worth it.

00:05:09.590 --> 00:05:10.690
So check it out.

00:05:10.690 --> 00:05:13.660
We have a link on the show
next to the blog post,

00:05:13.660 --> 00:05:16.000
but also to the GitHub repost.

00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:19.360
You can go and read the code
directly, which is very nice.

00:05:19.360 --> 00:05:20.110
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:05:20.110 --> 00:05:23.130
Well, why don't we go
talk to-- hee hee hee hee.

00:05:23.130 --> 00:05:26.082
Why don't we go talk
to Ed about Pokemon Go?

00:05:26.082 --> 00:05:27.290
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Sounds good.

00:05:27.290 --> 00:05:28.430
Let's do that.

00:05:28.430 --> 00:05:32.590
MARK MANDEL: So today, I am very
excited to have Edward Wu here

00:05:32.590 --> 00:05:35.470
from Niantic joining
us on the podcast.

00:05:35.470 --> 00:05:36.800
Ed, how are you doing today?

00:05:36.800 --> 00:05:37.190
EDWARD WU: Very good.

00:05:37.190 --> 00:05:38.860
Thank you very much
for having me here.

00:05:38.860 --> 00:05:41.260
I'm really excited to be
able to talk about how

00:05:41.260 --> 00:05:43.032
the past few months have gone.

00:05:43.032 --> 00:05:45.490
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, it's not
like you've been up to anything

00:05:45.490 --> 00:05:48.100
special or exciting, or anything
like that that anyone would

00:05:48.100 --> 00:05:49.240
have ever heard of or--

00:05:49.240 --> 00:05:49.600
EDWARD WU: Nothing big.

00:05:49.600 --> 00:05:50.641
MARK MANDEL: Nothing big.

00:05:50.641 --> 00:05:52.115
Nothing at all.

00:05:52.115 --> 00:05:54.240
EDWARD WU: Getting plenty
of sleep, of course, yes.

00:05:54.240 --> 00:05:54.830
MARK MANDEL: Of course.

00:05:54.830 --> 00:05:55.360
Excellent.

00:05:55.360 --> 00:05:58.525
Cool, so, before we go into
the magic of Pokemon Go,

00:05:58.525 --> 00:06:01.150
do you want to tell us a little
bit about you, and who you are,

00:06:01.150 --> 00:06:01.954
and what you do?

00:06:01.954 --> 00:06:02.620
EDWARD WU: Sure.

00:06:02.620 --> 00:06:05.770
I'm director of software
engineering here at Niantic.

00:06:05.770 --> 00:06:09.270
I actually lead the Pokemon
Go engineering team.

00:06:09.270 --> 00:06:15.520
Done so since we spun out of
Google in October of 2015,

00:06:15.520 --> 00:06:18.550
and all the way through
the launch of Pokemon Go

00:06:18.550 --> 00:06:20.690
last summer, all the
way through to today.

00:06:20.690 --> 00:06:23.930
And in addition to managing
the engineering and publication

00:06:23.930 --> 00:06:26.890
of Pokemon Go, I
also product manage

00:06:26.890 --> 00:06:29.051
portions of our technology
platform, which I'd

00:06:29.051 --> 00:06:30.300
be happy to speak about later.

00:06:30.300 --> 00:06:31.216
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:06:31.216 --> 00:06:34.510
So, I heard something
that surprised me

00:06:34.510 --> 00:06:36.490
in listening to one of
my favorite podcasts

00:06:36.490 --> 00:06:38.460
which is not this one.

00:06:38.460 --> 00:06:39.820
99% Invisible.

00:06:39.820 --> 00:06:43.240
I heard about this ship
below the [INAUDIBLE]

00:06:43.240 --> 00:06:46.330
here in San Francisco,
called the Niantic.

00:06:46.330 --> 00:06:47.650
Is there any relationship?

00:06:47.650 --> 00:06:50.800
EDWARD WU: Yeah, that's kind
of what our company is named

00:06:50.800 --> 00:06:53.140
after, and what our
autonomous unit while we were

00:06:53.140 --> 00:06:54.800
inside Google was named after.

00:06:54.800 --> 00:06:57.310
It's a ship that
actually ran aground--

00:06:57.310 --> 00:07:00.970
they ran aground during the
gold rush era of San Francisco.

00:07:00.970 --> 00:07:02.979
People were so excited to--

00:07:02.979 --> 00:07:05.020
I'm sure that they decided
just to run it aground

00:07:05.020 --> 00:07:07.510
and turn it into a hotel
rather than continuing

00:07:07.510 --> 00:07:09.740
to sail it back and forth.

00:07:09.740 --> 00:07:13.780
And I think the sense of
adventure, hence discovery,

00:07:13.780 --> 00:07:17.560
is part of what our
CEO and founder John

00:07:17.560 --> 00:07:18.700
was trying to evoke there.

00:07:18.700 --> 00:07:19.960
MARK MANDEL: So you
mentioned just a bit

00:07:19.960 --> 00:07:21.627
there, like, Niantic
spun out of Google.

00:07:21.627 --> 00:07:23.376
Do you want to give
us a little background

00:07:23.376 --> 00:07:24.459
of Niantic the company?

00:07:24.459 --> 00:07:26.500
I think it's got kind of
a little unique history.

00:07:26.500 --> 00:07:28.583
EDWARD WU: Yeah, it's got
a really unique history.

00:07:28.583 --> 00:07:30.890
So, our CEO and CTO--

00:07:30.890 --> 00:07:33.670
our CEO John Hanke and
our CTO Phil Keslin

00:07:33.670 --> 00:07:35.950
are actually veterans
of a previous startup

00:07:35.950 --> 00:07:38.230
that was acquired by
Google called Keyhole.

00:07:38.230 --> 00:07:40.270
Keyhole made a
product which turned

00:07:40.270 --> 00:07:42.690
into what most people know
as Google Earth today.

00:07:42.690 --> 00:07:46.276
And so while they were
in Google, they were--

00:07:46.276 --> 00:07:48.400
this was the age when, you
know, the very first set

00:07:48.400 --> 00:07:51.340
of smartphones began to
get out into the market,

00:07:51.340 --> 00:07:53.772
so things like Google's
Nexus One phone.

00:07:53.772 --> 00:07:55.480
And they looked at
these things, and they

00:07:55.480 --> 00:07:58.810
saw that the majority of
entertainment and experiences

00:07:58.810 --> 00:08:00.550
on them were things
like Angry Birds.

00:08:00.550 --> 00:08:03.790
Which, while it was novel from
like an input perspective,

00:08:03.790 --> 00:08:07.510
was not particularly making full
use of the insane capabilities

00:08:07.510 --> 00:08:10.836
that having a computer
carried around in your pocket

00:08:10.836 --> 00:08:11.710
could provide, right?

00:08:11.710 --> 00:08:14.770
These things are loaded with
location sensors, and network

00:08:14.770 --> 00:08:17.080
capabilities, and
advanced processing power.

00:08:17.080 --> 00:08:18.705
They really thought
there was something

00:08:18.705 --> 00:08:21.190
that they could do to
make unique experiences

00:08:21.190 --> 00:08:24.610
with these devices that,
since they were portable,

00:08:24.610 --> 00:08:27.334
actually got people outside,
got people to meet each other,

00:08:27.334 --> 00:08:28.750
got people to
explore their world,

00:08:28.750 --> 00:08:30.708
got people more engaged
with their communities.

00:08:30.708 --> 00:08:34.030
They started investigating
a variety of product ideas

00:08:34.030 --> 00:08:36.760
which could actually
go towards that goal.

00:08:36.760 --> 00:08:40.184
And they came up with two,
which are-- both were quite

00:08:40.184 --> 00:08:42.100
informative both from a
product and technology

00:08:42.100 --> 00:08:44.950
perspective for what eventually
would become Pokemon Go.

00:08:44.950 --> 00:08:47.390
So while we were in Google,
we launched two products.

00:08:47.390 --> 00:08:51.520
One is called Fieldtrip, which
used the location sensors

00:08:51.520 --> 00:08:54.730
on your phone to make local
recommendations of really

00:08:54.730 --> 00:08:58.030
interesting sites that
might be around you that you

00:08:58.030 --> 00:08:59.080
might want to check out.

00:08:59.080 --> 00:09:02.470
The other was a game called
Ingress, which is something

00:09:02.470 --> 00:09:05.530
that, if you play Pokemon
Go and then go back to,

00:09:05.530 --> 00:09:08.890
you'll recognize
large elements there.

00:09:08.890 --> 00:09:11.510
There's a resource collection
mechanic on the move.

00:09:11.510 --> 00:09:13.302
There are points of
interest in the game,

00:09:13.302 --> 00:09:15.010
that the game tries
to drive you towards.

00:09:15.010 --> 00:09:18.310
So, interesting local
historical monuments, or art,

00:09:18.310 --> 00:09:20.590
or small interesting
local businesses.

00:09:20.590 --> 00:09:23.710
And this game, just
like Pokemon Go,

00:09:23.710 --> 00:09:26.320
is a location based game
that tries to actually get

00:09:26.320 --> 00:09:29.320
people to interact out in
the real world together using

00:09:29.320 --> 00:09:31.390
a single consistent gameboard.

00:09:31.390 --> 00:09:35.890
And so we were actually
quite successful

00:09:35.890 --> 00:09:40.870
in getting a lot of
attention on Ingress.

00:09:40.870 --> 00:09:44.470
And one of the bits of
attention was actually

00:09:44.470 --> 00:09:46.600
the folks at the
Pokemon Company, who

00:09:46.600 --> 00:09:49.590
saw this game that was
actually getting people out

00:09:49.590 --> 00:09:51.370
into the real world,
that was actually

00:09:51.370 --> 00:09:54.544
getting them to meet others and
explore the local communities.

00:09:54.544 --> 00:09:56.710
It was actually kind of a
perfect fit, thematically,

00:09:56.710 --> 00:10:01.480
for the way that Pokemon has
been throughout its entire IP

00:10:01.480 --> 00:10:02.750
lifetime.

00:10:02.750 --> 00:10:07.510
And so, we decided
to spin out of Google

00:10:07.510 --> 00:10:10.370
as an independent
company, partnering

00:10:10.370 --> 00:10:13.700
with Nintendo and the Pokemon
Company to create Pokemon Go.

00:10:13.700 --> 00:10:16.490
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah, so,
you said that with Niantic you

00:10:16.490 --> 00:10:19.090
got a little bit of attention.

00:10:19.090 --> 00:10:21.830
I'd say that with Pokemon Go you
got a little bit of attention,

00:10:21.830 --> 00:10:23.240
too.

00:10:23.240 --> 00:10:24.770
It was kind of a big deal.

00:10:24.770 --> 00:10:28.760
Could you give us a little bit
an idea of how big of a deal

00:10:28.760 --> 00:10:29.330
it was.

00:10:29.330 --> 00:10:32.570
Like, we've heard everyone
saying that it was huge.

00:10:32.570 --> 00:10:34.010
How huge was it?

00:10:34.010 --> 00:10:36.020
EDWARD WU: Yeah,
so, as of December--

00:10:36.020 --> 00:10:38.600
I crunch the statistics
every so often with the help

00:10:38.600 --> 00:10:41.240
of the engineering team here.

00:10:41.240 --> 00:10:44.480
We take a look at not only the
kind of more technical numbers,

00:10:44.480 --> 00:10:46.520
but some product driven ones.

00:10:46.520 --> 00:10:49.960
So, we've actually had
people walk more than 8.7

00:10:49.960 --> 00:10:55.340
billion kilometers, which for
a sense of scale is past Pluto.

00:10:55.340 --> 00:10:59.640
And I think it's about 200,000
times around the Earth.

00:10:59.640 --> 00:11:04.070
And we've had 88-- more than
88 billion Pokemon caught

00:11:04.070 --> 00:11:06.020
by our players, collectively.

00:11:06.020 --> 00:11:08.540
So just to give you the
sheer sense of scale--

00:11:08.540 --> 00:11:11.545
I mean, it's almost
beyond comprehension.

00:11:11.545 --> 00:11:12.920
The collective
effort that people

00:11:12.920 --> 00:11:16.490
put into not only playing
this game, but actually,

00:11:16.490 --> 00:11:18.160
when you think about
all that walking,

00:11:18.160 --> 00:11:22.910
is the exercise and exploration
and meeting of new people

00:11:22.910 --> 00:11:25.490
that they've done
in the midst of it.

00:11:25.490 --> 00:11:27.855
From a more technical
perspective,

00:11:27.855 --> 00:11:31.820
to give you an idea of how
much bigger this was than what

00:11:31.820 --> 00:11:37.109
we expected, it was roughly 50
times greater than the capacity

00:11:37.109 --> 00:11:38.900
that we actually had
planned for at launch.

00:11:38.900 --> 00:11:41.420
And that's actually a story
I'd be happy to go into,

00:11:41.420 --> 00:11:42.485
because it was--

00:11:42.485 --> 00:11:45.320
it goes to the heart
of why the Google Cloud

00:11:45.320 --> 00:11:48.290
team was such an instrumental
part of our success.

00:11:48.290 --> 00:11:50.720
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So before
when we go into more details,

00:11:50.720 --> 00:11:54.260
I had a quick question above
those 88 billion Pokemon

00:11:54.260 --> 00:11:55.040
caught.

00:11:55.040 --> 00:11:58.001
How many of them were Pidgeys?

00:11:58.001 --> 00:12:00.375
EDWARD WU: I think a significant
fraction, but actually--

00:12:02.947 --> 00:12:05.030
people play the game for
different reasons, right?

00:12:05.030 --> 00:12:06.800
I think some people
go and they try

00:12:06.800 --> 00:12:08.630
to catch every Pidgey
in sight, because it

00:12:08.630 --> 00:12:10.880
gets them some stardust which
will allow them to power

00:12:10.880 --> 00:12:12.320
up their other Pokemon.

00:12:12.320 --> 00:12:14.950
Other people try
to pick and choose.

00:12:14.950 --> 00:12:16.446
So I don't think that the--

00:12:16.446 --> 00:12:18.332
the distribution that
you see on the screen

00:12:18.332 --> 00:12:19.790
is not necessarily
the distribution

00:12:19.790 --> 00:12:21.777
that people actually
end up catching.

00:12:21.777 --> 00:12:22.610
FRANCESC CAMPOY: OK.

00:12:22.610 --> 00:12:23.510
Interesting.

00:12:23.510 --> 00:12:23.960
MARK MANDEL: Excellent.

00:12:23.960 --> 00:12:24.210
All right.

00:12:24.210 --> 00:12:26.850
So, yeah, let's get stuck into
the technical side of these,

00:12:26.850 --> 00:12:29.330
because I think this is
absolutely fascinating.

00:12:29.330 --> 00:12:31.190
You mentioned, as well--

00:12:31.190 --> 00:12:33.110
obviously if you've
played in Ingress before,

00:12:33.110 --> 00:12:36.950
you can see elements
of that in Pokemon Go.

00:12:36.950 --> 00:12:39.866
Did you end up using the
same stuff that you used,

00:12:39.866 --> 00:12:41.490
the same products
that used in Ingress?

00:12:41.490 --> 00:12:43.397
Or did you take lessons
learned from Ingress

00:12:43.397 --> 00:12:44.480
and change certain things?

00:12:44.480 --> 00:12:46.563
Like, what was different
between the two products,

00:12:46.563 --> 00:12:49.100
and what lessons did
you learn between them?

00:12:49.100 --> 00:12:51.320
EDWARD WU: From a technical
architectural standpoint,

00:12:51.320 --> 00:12:52.695
the major transition
that we made

00:12:52.695 --> 00:12:56.510
was from App Engine, which was a
previous generation of Google's

00:12:56.510 --> 00:12:58.790
Cloud computing products,
to Google Container engine.

00:12:58.790 --> 00:13:02.360
Which was something that
only came out of a few years

00:13:02.360 --> 00:13:04.730
after Ingress had started.

00:13:04.730 --> 00:13:06.590
And so, we did take
the opportunity

00:13:06.590 --> 00:13:09.650
to modernize some of our
technical infrastructure.

00:13:09.650 --> 00:13:11.660
Be happy to go a
bit into that later.

00:13:11.660 --> 00:13:15.520
But many of the core algorithms,
and many of the key--

00:13:15.520 --> 00:13:18.470
both engineering learnings
that we took away

00:13:18.470 --> 00:13:22.670
from how Ingress operated, as
well as product lessons that we

00:13:22.670 --> 00:13:25.819
took away, greatly informed
how we implemented Pokemon Go.

00:13:25.819 --> 00:13:28.360
MARK MANDEL: So, you mentioned
sort of, you modernized stuff.

00:13:28.360 --> 00:13:30.526
I believe you are using
Kubernetes behind the scenes

00:13:30.526 --> 00:13:31.850
rather than the App Engine.

00:13:31.850 --> 00:13:32.475
EDWARD WU: Yes.

00:13:32.475 --> 00:13:34.640
MARK MANDEL: What
drove that decision?

00:13:34.640 --> 00:13:38.060
EDWARD WU: So, App Engine
came from a different era

00:13:38.060 --> 00:13:42.800
of Google's Cloud services,
where things ran in a sandbox.

00:13:42.800 --> 00:13:43.760
And it made it--

00:13:43.760 --> 00:13:45.710
App Engine, as a
product, is actually

00:13:45.710 --> 00:13:47.722
quite good for getting
something out the door

00:13:47.722 --> 00:13:48.680
really, really quickly.

00:13:48.680 --> 00:13:51.380
Not having to worry about
whether your front ends

00:13:51.380 --> 00:13:52.080
will scale.

00:13:52.080 --> 00:13:55.070
Not having to worry about
exactly what you're--

00:13:55.070 --> 00:13:57.890
having full control
of your service loop.

00:13:57.890 --> 00:14:01.690
It's kind of a plug and
play for web services.

00:14:01.690 --> 00:14:05.630
And so, we initially-- we
implemented Ingress on that

00:14:05.630 --> 00:14:08.900
because that was what Google
had available five years back

00:14:08.900 --> 00:14:09.680
or so.

00:14:09.680 --> 00:14:13.780
And it worked quite well for
us to begin to understand,

00:14:13.780 --> 00:14:17.660
how does running a game
where everybody in the world

00:14:17.660 --> 00:14:20.585
actually has to share a single
consistent view of that world?

00:14:20.585 --> 00:14:21.960
What kind of
technical trade-offs

00:14:21.960 --> 00:14:24.890
do you need to make in
order to make that happen

00:14:24.890 --> 00:14:27.770
in near real time, delivered
through the latency

00:14:27.770 --> 00:14:29.060
of a mobile connection?

00:14:29.060 --> 00:14:32.510
So App Engine was great for the
time that Ingress was launched.

00:14:32.510 --> 00:14:34.460
Now, as we moved
on to Pokemon Go,

00:14:34.460 --> 00:14:36.560
we wanted to modernize
our architecture a bit.

00:14:36.560 --> 00:14:40.287
And the major difference between
Kubernetes and App Engine

00:14:40.287 --> 00:14:41.870
is that Kubernetes
gives you something

00:14:41.870 --> 00:14:45.810
that's much closer to a
raw machine, in a sense.

00:14:45.810 --> 00:14:47.590
You can run your own--

00:14:47.590 --> 00:14:52.670
you can literally run Java Main
and start up your own server

00:14:52.670 --> 00:14:55.040
there, and take inbound
network traffic,

00:14:55.040 --> 00:14:57.530
and configure it to your liking.

00:14:57.530 --> 00:15:00.650
In fact, we spun out of Google.

00:15:00.650 --> 00:15:02.480
Many of us are Google engineers.

00:15:02.480 --> 00:15:05.460
And Kubernetes actually
reassembles, very,

00:15:05.460 --> 00:15:08.030
very strongly, the kind
of internal infrastructure

00:15:08.030 --> 00:15:10.060
that we were used to
as Google engineers.

00:15:10.060 --> 00:15:14.270
All of the ability to
define services that

00:15:14.270 --> 00:15:18.242
ran very close to Bare Metal.

00:15:18.242 --> 00:15:19.700
It had all the
flexibility of being

00:15:19.700 --> 00:15:21.950
able to interact very
closely with the operating

00:15:21.950 --> 00:15:24.470
system and the network stack.

00:15:24.470 --> 00:15:28.640
But still was abstracted enough
away from the Bare Metal,

00:15:28.640 --> 00:15:32.810
such that we could rapidly
scale the number of instances

00:15:32.810 --> 00:15:33.900
if necessary.

00:15:33.900 --> 00:15:36.770
So what Kubernetes eventually,
and Google Container Engine

00:15:36.770 --> 00:15:40.130
gave us, was all
that flexibility

00:15:40.130 --> 00:15:42.980
of scaling and deployment
that App Engine did,

00:15:42.980 --> 00:15:45.410
plus much more technical
flexibility for us

00:15:45.410 --> 00:15:49.120
to define our own binaries.

00:15:49.120 --> 00:15:50.090
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:15:50.090 --> 00:15:54.890
So, keeping on the
topic of your stack,

00:15:54.890 --> 00:15:57.820
you mentioned Java
Main which kind of

00:15:57.820 --> 00:15:59.360
made me sad, because
I was wondering

00:15:59.360 --> 00:16:01.010
if Pokemon Go was
actually written

00:16:01.010 --> 00:16:03.130
in Go in any piece of it.

00:16:03.130 --> 00:16:04.130
EDWARD WU: Just the HUD.

00:16:04.130 --> 00:16:07.232
Just for the name
of the product.

00:16:07.232 --> 00:16:08.690
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Could you tell us,

00:16:08.690 --> 00:16:10.430
is everything written in Java?

00:16:10.430 --> 00:16:12.320
What languages are you using?

00:16:12.320 --> 00:16:15.140
EDWARD WU: So, the server
stack is more or less Java.

00:16:15.140 --> 00:16:20.360
And, as former Google engineers,
and being part of the industry

00:16:20.360 --> 00:16:23.900
standard, Java really I think
was the right decision for us

00:16:23.900 --> 00:16:24.540
to make.

00:16:24.540 --> 00:16:27.290
It allowed us to iterate
very, very quickly

00:16:27.290 --> 00:16:30.740
on gameplay concepts, as well
as bring in existing code

00:16:30.740 --> 00:16:33.680
that we had from
Ingress and port it over

00:16:33.680 --> 00:16:36.560
from App Engine to
Container Engine,

00:16:36.560 --> 00:16:38.950
since Java was one of the
supported languages on App

00:16:38.950 --> 00:16:39.650
Engine.

00:16:39.650 --> 00:16:42.050
So, the other thing that
Java allowed us to do

00:16:42.050 --> 00:16:45.410
was, because it's an industry
standard language, was

00:16:45.410 --> 00:16:48.230
it allowed us to bring the
best and brightest of server

00:16:48.230 --> 00:16:51.380
engineers who wanted to work
on a product like Pokemon Go

00:16:51.380 --> 00:16:52.384
into Niantic.

00:16:52.384 --> 00:16:53.300
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:16:53.300 --> 00:16:56.810
So, one of the things
that surprised me

00:16:56.810 --> 00:16:59.990
was, I saw that you
were using Datastore.

00:16:59.990 --> 00:17:04.160
And at the same time, you have
the biggest Kubernetes cluster

00:17:04.160 --> 00:17:04.710
in the world.

00:17:04.710 --> 00:17:07.160
Or at least, when we announced
it, it was the biggest one.

00:17:07.160 --> 00:17:08.500
MARK MANDEL: As far as
we were aware, anyway.

00:17:08.500 --> 00:17:09.530
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So
I was wondering--

00:17:09.530 --> 00:17:11.540
I mean, you definitely
had space to run

00:17:11.540 --> 00:17:14.349
the database on the Kubernetes
cluster, it's so big.

00:17:14.349 --> 00:17:15.500
Why didn't you do it?

00:17:15.500 --> 00:17:17.776
Why did you go with Datastore?

00:17:17.776 --> 00:17:19.609
EDWARD WU: So, there's
some prior experience

00:17:19.609 --> 00:17:20.480
that we had with Datastore.

00:17:20.480 --> 00:17:21.771
But that was not determinative.

00:17:21.771 --> 00:17:26.359
So, Datastore was pretty much
our only option for Ingress,

00:17:26.359 --> 00:17:29.570
because again, App Engine is
this quick framework, which

00:17:29.570 --> 00:17:33.120
allows you to not have to worry
about implementing a database,

00:17:33.120 --> 00:17:37.850
doesn't make you worry about
setting up of your own web

00:17:37.850 --> 00:17:39.800
servlet binary et cetera.

00:17:39.800 --> 00:17:42.584
But, the price that
you pay is that you

00:17:42.584 --> 00:17:44.750
have to go with the chosen
storage technology, which

00:17:44.750 --> 00:17:45.900
in that case was Datastore.

00:17:45.900 --> 00:17:49.420
Now, that being said,
Datastore was an--

00:17:49.420 --> 00:17:51.230
is an awesome technology.

00:17:51.230 --> 00:17:53.270
And that's why we
actually-- when

00:17:53.270 --> 00:17:57.590
we made the transition from
App Engine to Container Engine,

00:17:57.590 --> 00:17:59.590
we evaluated a variety
of different options.

00:17:59.590 --> 00:18:04.250
Being able to stand up our
own relational database,

00:18:04.250 --> 00:18:07.130
or using some other
cloud based [INAUDIBLE]

00:18:07.130 --> 00:18:10.040
provider was definitely other
options that we looked at.

00:18:10.040 --> 00:18:11.800
But we kept coming
back to Datastore.

00:18:11.800 --> 00:18:15.230
And the reason that we
stuck with Datastore

00:18:15.230 --> 00:18:18.200
was because it scales
almost indefinitely

00:18:18.200 --> 00:18:20.000
from doing individual
development

00:18:20.000 --> 00:18:22.070
as an individual developer
on a test instance,

00:18:22.070 --> 00:18:25.250
all the way up to games at
the scale of Pokemon Go.

00:18:25.250 --> 00:18:26.360
And it scales seamlessly.

00:18:26.360 --> 00:18:28.820
You don't need to do any
configuration changes.

00:18:28.820 --> 00:18:30.890
At least on our side,
on the developer side,

00:18:30.890 --> 00:18:32.848
we didn't need to do any
configuration changes.

00:18:32.848 --> 00:18:34.880
We didn't need to do
any kind of futzing

00:18:34.880 --> 00:18:39.280
with routing, or futzing with
scaling the database back ends,

00:18:39.280 --> 00:18:40.100
or anything.

00:18:40.100 --> 00:18:42.470
All we had to do was
program against the set

00:18:42.470 --> 00:18:44.130
of transactional APIs.

00:18:44.130 --> 00:18:49.070
And what we got in return
was multi NTT transactions

00:18:49.070 --> 00:18:51.380
that were consistent
and distributed.

00:18:51.380 --> 00:18:53.825
That we didn't have to
worry about making sure

00:18:53.825 --> 00:18:56.120
were backed up or replicated.

00:18:56.120 --> 00:18:59.630
And advanced indexing
capabilities which go to--

00:18:59.630 --> 00:19:03.830
which we use at both the heart
of both Ingress and Pokemon Go.

00:19:03.830 --> 00:19:06.950
And so after taking a
look at a lot of options--

00:19:06.950 --> 00:19:10.950
maybe using like a
sorted MySQL instance,

00:19:10.950 --> 00:19:15.800
or using a different third
party NoSQL provider.

00:19:15.800 --> 00:19:17.870
We really thought
that Datastore had

00:19:17.870 --> 00:19:21.650
the most advanced combination
of the scale of the multi NTT

00:19:21.650 --> 00:19:24.260
transactions for
a NoSQL datastore,

00:19:24.260 --> 00:19:27.470
as well as advanced indexing
capabilities and ease

00:19:27.470 --> 00:19:30.620
of use and maintenance that
were just unparalleled.

00:19:30.620 --> 00:19:32.972
And frankly, were a key
part of our success.

00:19:32.972 --> 00:19:33.930
MARK MANDEL: Excellent.

00:19:33.930 --> 00:19:35.450
Well, that's super cool.

00:19:35.450 --> 00:19:38.346
So, I know we've talked
about GKE and Datastore.

00:19:38.346 --> 00:19:40.220
And those are the only
products you're using?

00:19:40.220 --> 00:19:41.690
Are you able to talk
about anything else

00:19:41.690 --> 00:19:43.689
that you've used that's
on Google Cloud Platform

00:19:43.689 --> 00:19:44.645
or across Google?

00:19:44.645 --> 00:19:45.770
EDWARD WU: Yes, absolutely.

00:19:45.770 --> 00:19:49.994
So, one of the benefits of
having worked at Google,

00:19:49.994 --> 00:19:51.410
and being former
Google engineers,

00:19:51.410 --> 00:19:54.740
is that we've actually seen
the progression of tools

00:19:54.740 --> 00:19:57.680
that Google created for its
own internal engineering

00:19:57.680 --> 00:20:01.316
progressively propagated out as
Google Cloud Platform products.

00:20:01.316 --> 00:20:03.440
And one of the ones that
I really want to highlight

00:20:03.440 --> 00:20:05.830
is Google Cloud Bigtable.

00:20:05.830 --> 00:20:07.870
We loved working
with of Bigtable

00:20:07.870 --> 00:20:09.970
as engineers within Google.

00:20:09.970 --> 00:20:12.160
It was-- it's a key part
of many, many, many Google

00:20:12.160 --> 00:20:13.030
systems.

00:20:13.030 --> 00:20:15.880
And its debut as
an external service

00:20:15.880 --> 00:20:19.090
is for completely different
use cases that for Datastore.

00:20:19.090 --> 00:20:21.910
Datastore we use
for transactions,

00:20:21.910 --> 00:20:25.660
whereas Bigtable is more for
bulk storage and bulk data

00:20:25.660 --> 00:20:26.500
processing.

00:20:26.500 --> 00:20:28.737
And it's been an
incredible product

00:20:28.737 --> 00:20:30.070
to actually have on the outside.

00:20:30.070 --> 00:20:33.400
And I hope that Google
continues to push

00:20:33.400 --> 00:20:36.280
more and more of the
awesome internal tools

00:20:36.280 --> 00:20:39.840
that we loved as
engineers in Google,

00:20:39.840 --> 00:20:41.630
out to the Cloud platform.

00:20:41.630 --> 00:20:43.960
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah, we
cannot confirm nor deny.

00:20:43.960 --> 00:20:45.350
But, yes.

00:20:45.350 --> 00:20:48.150
I've think that there's
more coming, yeah.

00:20:48.150 --> 00:20:49.060
EDWARD WU: Awesome.

00:20:49.060 --> 00:20:50.720
MARK MANDEL: OK, cool.

00:20:50.720 --> 00:20:52.960
This'll probably segue
actually quite nicely to,

00:20:52.960 --> 00:20:55.990
you talked about how
with Datastore, you

00:20:55.990 --> 00:20:59.530
didn't have to do any changes to
handle the amount of throughput

00:20:59.530 --> 00:21:00.530
that was coming through.

00:21:00.530 --> 00:21:02.785
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah, that's a
very subtle way of putting it.

00:21:02.785 --> 00:21:04.409
MARK MANDEL: I thought
that was quite--

00:21:04.409 --> 00:21:06.490
I thought that was quite cute.

00:21:06.490 --> 00:21:10.600
But I know we have announced the
Cluster Reliability Engineering

00:21:10.600 --> 00:21:13.360
Team, the CRE Team,
and they basically

00:21:13.360 --> 00:21:16.720
started interacting
with you as a customer.

00:21:16.720 --> 00:21:18.184
How was that experience?

00:21:18.184 --> 00:21:19.100
Like, how did that go?

00:21:19.100 --> 00:21:21.550
What was that like?

00:21:21.550 --> 00:21:23.740
EDWARD WU: So, I think here
is actually a good time

00:21:23.740 --> 00:21:25.573
to go through the whole
story of the launch,

00:21:25.573 --> 00:21:31.720
because it's directly relevant
to how that interaction was

00:21:31.720 --> 00:21:33.100
so helpful.

00:21:33.100 --> 00:21:36.760
So, we did our homework
before we launched Pokemon Go.

00:21:36.760 --> 00:21:37.480
I swear we did.

00:21:37.480 --> 00:21:39.730
We went and we looked
at the launches

00:21:39.730 --> 00:21:40.964
for other mobile games.

00:21:40.964 --> 00:21:43.255
Top two mobile games, right,
ones with established IPs,

00:21:43.255 --> 00:21:45.850
ones that were sequels
of really popular games

00:21:45.850 --> 00:21:47.650
in the past couple of years.

00:21:47.650 --> 00:21:48.765
And we looked at--

00:21:48.765 --> 00:21:51.350
from publicly available
business intelligence,

00:21:51.350 --> 00:21:53.532
what the number of--

00:21:53.532 --> 00:21:55.240
what their growth
curves would look like.

00:21:55.240 --> 00:21:57.650
What the number of users that
they would have would look

00:21:57.650 --> 00:21:58.150
like.

00:21:58.150 --> 00:22:01.310
And we used those numbers,
and our experience on Ingress,

00:22:01.310 --> 00:22:04.150
to extrapolate from the
product driven numbers,

00:22:04.150 --> 00:22:06.000
like monthly and
daily active users,

00:22:06.000 --> 00:22:09.040
to what we thought would
be the load on our servers

00:22:09.040 --> 00:22:12.452
and on Cloud Datastore
at any given time.

00:22:12.452 --> 00:22:14.660
And our Cloud Datastore team
leading up to our launch

00:22:14.660 --> 00:22:15.159
was great.

00:22:15.159 --> 00:22:17.140
They were constantly
in touch with us,

00:22:17.140 --> 00:22:21.250
helping us with upgrades to
the APIs, or fixes for things

00:22:21.250 --> 00:22:24.430
that we didn't understand
when we encountered them.

00:22:24.430 --> 00:22:28.090
And they also asked,
quite reasonably,

00:22:28.090 --> 00:22:30.610
so what do you think your
capacity for launch will be?

00:22:30.610 --> 00:22:34.360
So we extrapolated it from all
of those numbers, to what we

00:22:34.360 --> 00:22:36.880
thought was a reasonable number.

00:22:36.880 --> 00:22:38.980
And then we multiplied
the reasonable number

00:22:38.980 --> 00:22:41.890
by like five times, and
said this is ludicrous.

00:22:41.890 --> 00:22:46.810
Nobody will ever have to go
five times bigger than anything

00:22:46.810 --> 00:22:48.790
else that's out there.

00:22:48.790 --> 00:22:52.960
And we'll ask Datastore
to set that aside for us.

00:22:52.960 --> 00:22:57.820
So we launched the game on
the evening of July 5th.

00:22:57.820 --> 00:23:00.370
So July 4th is, of course, a
holiday in the United States.

00:23:00.370 --> 00:23:03.250
We were lucky enough to actually
get everything squared away

00:23:03.250 --> 00:23:05.300
before the holiday weekend.

00:23:05.300 --> 00:23:09.561
And so I had everybody go
and have barbecues and watch

00:23:09.561 --> 00:23:10.810
fireworks with their families.

00:23:10.810 --> 00:23:14.920
And we came in rearing on
July 5th, ready to launch.

00:23:14.920 --> 00:23:16.840
And the way that we
were going to launch

00:23:16.840 --> 00:23:19.510
was that we started out with
Australia and New Zealand.

00:23:19.510 --> 00:23:22.480
These are markets
that we could watch

00:23:22.480 --> 00:23:26.890
that were in another time
zone, and that we could

00:23:26.890 --> 00:23:29.860
see through the evening as to
how they progressed before we

00:23:29.860 --> 00:23:33.610
moved on to more populated areas
of the world the United States.

00:23:33.610 --> 00:23:37.190
And what ended up happening
was, we immediately-- like,

00:23:37.190 --> 00:23:40.560
it looks like a delta
function spike, right?

00:23:40.560 --> 00:23:44.710
Like, we immediately
blew past the 1x number,

00:23:44.710 --> 00:23:47.224
the kind of baseline
estimate for the whole world

00:23:47.224 --> 00:23:48.640
that we thought
we were going to--

00:23:48.640 --> 00:23:50.114
we were going to be at, right?

00:23:50.114 --> 00:23:51.530
Just in Australia
and New Zealand.

00:23:51.530 --> 00:23:56.440
And we were well on our way to
that ludicrous 5x number by--

00:23:56.440 --> 00:23:58.750
just from just from
Australia and New Zealand.

00:23:58.750 --> 00:24:00.580
Mind you, Australia
and New Zealand

00:24:00.580 --> 00:24:04.570
we expected, from other product
launches of other business

00:24:04.570 --> 00:24:06.130
intelligence in
the mobile market,

00:24:06.130 --> 00:24:08.620
to be single digit
percentages, at most,

00:24:08.620 --> 00:24:11.830
of the entire world
wide active user base.

00:24:11.830 --> 00:24:16.270
And it is at this point that we
call up our friends at Google

00:24:16.270 --> 00:24:18.970
and said, well, you know those
estimates that we gave you?

00:24:18.970 --> 00:24:21.970
Few Those were great.

00:24:21.970 --> 00:24:24.170
We might need to go
a little bit higher.

00:24:24.170 --> 00:24:24.974
MARK MANDEL: Rats.

00:24:24.974 --> 00:24:25.640
EDWARD WU: Haha.

00:24:25.640 --> 00:24:29.350
And it turned out that over the
course of the next few weeks

00:24:29.350 --> 00:24:31.349
as we launched across all of--

00:24:31.349 --> 00:24:33.640
first the United States the
day after Australia and New

00:24:33.640 --> 00:24:36.720
Zealand, and then progressively
around the rest of the world,

00:24:36.720 --> 00:24:39.110
that we ended up eventually
needing something

00:24:39.110 --> 00:24:42.640
on order of 50 times
the initial capacity

00:24:42.640 --> 00:24:44.999
estimate so we gave
to Cloud Datastore.

00:24:44.999 --> 00:24:46.290
MARK MANDEL: Just a little bit.

00:24:46.290 --> 00:24:47.623
EDWARD WU: So just a little bit.

00:24:47.623 --> 00:24:51.747
So, that team was instrumental
in working with us to make sure

00:24:51.747 --> 00:24:52.705
that that could happen.

00:24:52.705 --> 00:24:56.260
I know that pulling
that off, basically

00:24:56.260 --> 00:24:59.400
within two weeks between
our initial launches

00:24:59.400 --> 00:25:02.817
to launch in Japan
and then further

00:25:02.817 --> 00:25:04.900
on into the rest of the
month throughout Southeast

00:25:04.900 --> 00:25:10.660
Asia and Latin America-- pulling
that off required a lot of work

00:25:10.660 --> 00:25:13.090
on the Google side.

00:25:13.090 --> 00:25:14.860
I don't think that
they'd ever seen

00:25:14.860 --> 00:25:20.200
anything of this magnitude
come through a single customer

00:25:20.200 --> 00:25:21.170
service.

00:25:21.170 --> 00:25:23.500
And so I know that
they did a lot of work

00:25:23.500 --> 00:25:25.960
to rearrange internal
resources, and move things

00:25:25.960 --> 00:25:27.668
around in their data
centers to make sure

00:25:27.668 --> 00:25:31.180
that they could support
us all along the way.

00:25:31.180 --> 00:25:35.032
And during all of that, we
didn't have to-- like I said,

00:25:35.032 --> 00:25:37.240
we didn't have to change
anything in our APIs, right?

00:25:37.240 --> 00:25:40.510
We didn't have to reconfigure
any kind of routing system.

00:25:40.510 --> 00:25:43.570
Or, we didn't have to change
the type of transactions.

00:25:43.570 --> 00:25:46.090
We just kept on plugging on
doing what we were doing,

00:25:46.090 --> 00:25:49.930
fixing our bugs,
and fixing anything

00:25:49.930 --> 00:25:52.600
that we had in our own
servers, that we had

00:25:52.600 --> 00:25:54.280
to work out to get to scale.

00:25:54.280 --> 00:25:57.826
And the Datastore
team did everything

00:25:57.826 --> 00:25:59.510
that they could to support us.

00:25:59.510 --> 00:26:02.980
And so one of the things I'm
really proud of, is that--

00:26:02.980 --> 00:26:06.490
in fact, I think the separation
was actually full and complete.

00:26:06.490 --> 00:26:08.800
Google engineers never took
a look into our code base.

00:26:08.800 --> 00:26:09.841
We never invited them to.

00:26:09.841 --> 00:26:11.960
They never needed to.

00:26:11.960 --> 00:26:17.920
The layer of the API abstraction
was complete and perfect.

00:26:17.920 --> 00:26:21.430
And I'm very proud of how we
eventually ended up scaling.

00:26:21.430 --> 00:26:23.440
I know the first few
days, it was obviously

00:26:23.440 --> 00:26:26.320
a little difficult to
get into the servers.

00:26:26.320 --> 00:26:28.690
But it turns out that
our fundamental technical

00:26:28.690 --> 00:26:30.010
architecture was sound.

00:26:30.010 --> 00:26:33.850
And what our scaling bugs
were, were the kinds of things

00:26:33.850 --> 00:26:36.580
that you'd normally run
across as you scale up

00:26:36.580 --> 00:26:38.380
any web service.

00:26:38.380 --> 00:26:42.940
So, typical debugging
looped looked to us like,

00:26:42.940 --> 00:26:47.620
OK, well let's take a dump
of all the stack traces that

00:26:47.620 --> 00:26:49.830
are running in the
server right now.

00:26:49.830 --> 00:26:52.450
And oh, like maybe 75%
of them are caught up

00:26:52.450 --> 00:26:54.100
on some silly mutex somewhere.

00:26:54.100 --> 00:26:57.760
So you go and you do a full on
code change to fix the mutex.

00:26:57.760 --> 00:26:59.950
And then throughput
shoots up on the server,

00:26:59.950 --> 00:27:01.960
and then look for
the next bottleneck.

00:27:01.960 --> 00:27:05.450
These are things that are very,
very routine for people who

00:27:05.450 --> 00:27:07.390
deploy web services that scale.

00:27:07.390 --> 00:27:09.280
The only difference
between them and us,

00:27:09.280 --> 00:27:11.490
is that we had to do so on
the back of a rocket ship.

00:27:15.490 --> 00:27:18.899
I think other folks get a little
bit of a gentler growth curve.

00:27:18.899 --> 00:27:21.190
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah, I have
an article right now next

00:27:21.190 --> 00:27:25.960
to me saying that after
day two, you basically

00:27:25.960 --> 00:27:30.670
reached the daily users of
Twitter, which is crazy.

00:27:30.670 --> 00:27:33.610
Like, the amount of users that
you got so fast and scaling

00:27:33.610 --> 00:27:38.740
that fast without having
to rewrite all your code.

00:27:38.740 --> 00:27:41.170
I'm pretty sure that if
I have any of my demos

00:27:41.170 --> 00:27:44.220
have to scale that much,
better rewrite the whole thing.

00:27:44.220 --> 00:27:46.320
So that is pretty amazing.

00:27:46.320 --> 00:27:48.602
EDWARD WU: Yeah, I can't
talk about daily users

00:27:48.602 --> 00:27:49.810
in particular, unfortunately.

00:27:49.810 --> 00:27:53.860
But obviously if I may,
one of the other key points

00:27:53.860 --> 00:27:57.100
of the way that we scale
that I'm very, very proud of

00:27:57.100 --> 00:28:00.190
is that these are just
isolated users working

00:28:00.190 --> 00:28:02.320
in their own instance.

00:28:02.320 --> 00:28:04.930
I think-- we'll get to the key
of why this is so important

00:28:04.930 --> 00:28:05.470
later--

00:28:05.470 --> 00:28:08.080
but ultimately Pokemon Go
a single consistent world

00:28:08.080 --> 00:28:10.930
for everybody who plays on it.

00:28:10.930 --> 00:28:12.490
If somebody
manipulates a Pokestop

00:28:12.490 --> 00:28:16.420
and puts a lure on there,
everybody within about a

00:28:16.420 --> 00:28:18.760
kilometer has to see
those pedals dropping

00:28:18.760 --> 00:28:20.230
from the Pokestop.

00:28:20.230 --> 00:28:26.200
If a few dozen people are
in the same park together

00:28:26.200 --> 00:28:28.852
and there's a Squirtle
on one end of the park

00:28:28.852 --> 00:28:30.310
and they're all
within range of it,

00:28:30.310 --> 00:28:33.700
they all have to be able to see
that Squirtle at the same time.

00:28:33.700 --> 00:28:35.530
Maintaining that
consistent world state

00:28:35.530 --> 00:28:38.590
means that we have a variety
of, not just n scaling problems,

00:28:38.590 --> 00:28:41.180
but n squared like
scaling problems.

00:28:41.180 --> 00:28:44.920
I'm very proud that not only our
algorithms, but Google's Cloud

00:28:44.920 --> 00:28:47.470
Platform, were
able to accommodate

00:28:47.470 --> 00:28:50.200
those algorithms to scale
in such a fashion, which

00:28:50.200 --> 00:28:53.620
I think is really a non-trivial
feat of engineering.

00:28:53.620 --> 00:28:57.370
And it is entirely
empowered by the fact

00:28:57.370 --> 00:29:00.010
that we were running on
Container Engine and Datastore,

00:29:00.010 --> 00:29:04.120
and were able to basically
turn the knobs as necessary

00:29:04.120 --> 00:29:05.620
with our binaries
on those platforms

00:29:05.620 --> 00:29:08.792
in order to make that
n squared scaling work.

00:29:08.792 --> 00:29:09.750
MARK MANDEL: Excellent.

00:29:09.750 --> 00:29:11.350
I'm sure I'm going
to get deflected,

00:29:11.350 --> 00:29:13.810
but I feel like I
should ask anyway.

00:29:13.810 --> 00:29:17.140
So what can we expect for
the future of Pokemon Go?

00:29:17.140 --> 00:29:18.640
EDWARD WU: So,
unfortunately I can't

00:29:18.640 --> 00:29:21.940
talk about any particular
future product launches.

00:29:21.940 --> 00:29:25.260
But we are working very,
very heavily on really,

00:29:25.260 --> 00:29:28.520
a set of features that,
frankly, as a player,

00:29:28.520 --> 00:29:31.000
I'm insanely excited about.

00:29:31.000 --> 00:29:33.430
I will say that Ingress
is about four years old.

00:29:33.430 --> 00:29:36.950
And during that time, we
have improved substantially.

00:29:36.950 --> 00:29:38.680
I think in vast
swaths of the game

00:29:38.680 --> 00:29:40.360
actually were not
there at launch.

00:29:40.360 --> 00:29:42.370
So, things like the
Ingress mission system,

00:29:42.370 --> 00:29:47.680
or the regional scoring system,
or player profile and badges.

00:29:47.680 --> 00:29:49.750
All of this stuff was not
there when we initially

00:29:49.750 --> 00:29:50.470
launched Ingress.

00:29:50.470 --> 00:29:52.930
And we are committed to
these games for years,

00:29:52.930 --> 00:29:54.549
because we know from Ingress--

00:29:54.549 --> 00:29:56.590
we know that people actually
play them for years.

00:29:56.590 --> 00:30:00.500
And so I'm very, very excited
to follow a similar trajectory

00:30:00.500 --> 00:30:04.030
on Pokemon Go, to continue
to support and build up

00:30:04.030 --> 00:30:08.440
major features in this game and
bring a lot more joy to people

00:30:08.440 --> 00:30:09.885
as time goes on.

00:30:09.885 --> 00:30:11.260
MARK MANDEL: So,
you've obviously

00:30:11.260 --> 00:30:15.970
got a history of building mobile
location based experiences.

00:30:15.970 --> 00:30:17.680
Is there plans to
expand upon that?

00:30:17.680 --> 00:30:19.390
More products,
that sort of thing?

00:30:19.390 --> 00:30:20.920
EDWARD WU: Absolutely.

00:30:20.920 --> 00:30:24.700
So one of the great things
about moving to Container Engine

00:30:24.700 --> 00:30:26.500
was that we took--

00:30:26.500 --> 00:30:29.440
we took the opportunity while
we did so to also separate

00:30:29.440 --> 00:30:32.920
our game logic from what I call
our core geospatial inquiry

00:30:32.920 --> 00:30:33.940
logic.

00:30:33.940 --> 00:30:36.160
And the Core geospatial
inquiry logic

00:30:36.160 --> 00:30:39.220
and the way that we interact
with Google's Cloud platforms

00:30:39.220 --> 00:30:42.340
are segregated into
a separate codebase.

00:30:42.340 --> 00:30:44.210
Literally, we run to repos.

00:30:44.210 --> 00:30:47.450
A platform repo, and then
a game specific repo.

00:30:47.450 --> 00:30:47.950
So--

00:30:47.950 --> 00:30:48.460
MARK MANDEL: Interesting.

00:30:48.460 --> 00:30:50.168
EDWARD WU: We very
much want to make sure

00:30:50.168 --> 00:30:53.440
that the way that we interact
with the Cloud platform

00:30:53.440 --> 00:30:58.270
is nicely encapsulated, so that
we can very quickly scale out

00:30:58.270 --> 00:31:00.760
our own internal teams to
try a variety of really

00:31:00.760 --> 00:31:03.260
interesting new product ideas.

00:31:03.260 --> 00:31:06.850
And we really believe that this
is a genre, an entire genre

00:31:06.850 --> 00:31:09.340
of games that
actually is prob-- is

00:31:09.340 --> 00:31:13.200
the future of what mobile and
AR gaming is going to look like.

00:31:13.200 --> 00:31:15.340
And there are a lot of
great ideas out there.

00:31:15.340 --> 00:31:19.420
And in order for us to be able
to rapidly iterate and try

00:31:19.420 --> 00:31:21.880
to winnow them down
into our next product,

00:31:21.880 --> 00:31:25.000
we absolutely need to be
able to abstract away the way

00:31:25.000 --> 00:31:28.100
that we interact with Cloud
platform technologies.

00:31:28.100 --> 00:31:30.760
And so, in the
creation of Pokemon Go,

00:31:30.760 --> 00:31:33.010
we already took on
the vast majority

00:31:33.010 --> 00:31:34.730
of the work necessary to do so.

00:31:34.730 --> 00:31:37.490
And I'm really excited
to see what we're

00:31:37.490 --> 00:31:40.030
going to come up with next.

00:31:40.030 --> 00:31:42.740
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So, in
a more personal note,

00:31:42.740 --> 00:31:46.450
I know that for me, Pokemon Go
actually changed a little bit

00:31:46.450 --> 00:31:49.390
the way that-- like, I would
take the bus less often,

00:31:49.390 --> 00:31:51.940
because the best goes too
fast, you cannot really catch

00:31:51.940 --> 00:31:53.110
the Pokemon that easy.

00:31:53.110 --> 00:31:55.140
So I've got walk
a little bit more.

00:31:55.140 --> 00:31:55.810
What about you?

00:31:55.810 --> 00:31:57.910
Is there-- like, I'm
curious about you,

00:31:57.910 --> 00:32:00.250
since you lived it
from the inside.

00:32:00.250 --> 00:32:03.460
What did Pokemon Go
change in your life?

00:32:03.460 --> 00:32:06.910
EDWARD WU: Yeah, so, I will
say, on a personal note

00:32:06.910 --> 00:32:11.710
my father plays this
game religiously.

00:32:11.710 --> 00:32:15.640
And he's actually a level 31
trainer, which is amazing to me

00:32:15.640 --> 00:32:19.570
because he hardly ever played
video games ever in the past.

00:32:19.570 --> 00:32:22.990
And this is an incredibly
gratifying for me

00:32:22.990 --> 00:32:25.440
personally, because
for the last,

00:32:25.440 --> 00:32:29.230
I'd say dozen or
so years, he's been

00:32:29.230 --> 00:32:33.670
managing his blood sugar
intake because of adult onset

00:32:33.670 --> 00:32:34.750
diabetes.

00:32:34.750 --> 00:32:39.390
And this game has gotten him
out walking daily, exploring

00:32:39.390 --> 00:32:42.130
his neighborhood,
keeping up his exercise.

00:32:42.130 --> 00:32:45.190
And it's gotten his
blood sugar to be as rock

00:32:45.190 --> 00:32:47.860
solid and stable as ever.

00:32:47.860 --> 00:32:49.690
I mean, it's incredible
to be able to have

00:32:49.690 --> 00:32:51.020
that kind of effect.

00:32:51.020 --> 00:32:54.730
And if you think about--
that's my father, of course.

00:32:54.730 --> 00:32:58.240
That's incredibly near and
dear and personal to me.

00:32:58.240 --> 00:33:01.690
But it's an incredibly
gratifying as an engineer

00:33:01.690 --> 00:33:04.810
to realize that there must be
millions of people like him

00:33:04.810 --> 00:33:07.390
out there, who are playing
this game, who are improving

00:33:07.390 --> 00:33:09.640
their health, who
are finding out

00:33:09.640 --> 00:33:13.210
more about their communities,
all because of code that--

00:33:13.210 --> 00:33:16.100
not only that we wrote, but
that Google's Cloud Platform

00:33:16.100 --> 00:33:17.440
empowers.

00:33:17.440 --> 00:33:19.900
Ultimately, Google making
these services available

00:33:19.900 --> 00:33:23.020
is a paradigm shift in the way
that single consistent world

00:33:23.020 --> 00:33:25.570
gaming can actually
be engineered,

00:33:25.570 --> 00:33:29.390
and can actually be deployed
and delivered to mobile phones.

00:33:29.390 --> 00:33:32.680
And it's amazing to think
that we live in an era

00:33:32.680 --> 00:33:36.570
where we can actually
drive substantial changes

00:33:36.570 --> 00:33:38.080
in personal behavior
for the better,

00:33:38.080 --> 00:33:41.590
based on what people
might think of as

00:33:41.590 --> 00:33:46.330
dry technological concepts
like Container Engine.

00:33:46.330 --> 00:33:48.972
When in fact, it's
actually quite exciting.

00:33:48.972 --> 00:33:49.930
MARK MANDEL: Excellent.

00:33:49.930 --> 00:33:51.820
That makes a lot of
sense, and I can totally

00:33:51.820 --> 00:33:54.550
see why that is
very exciting, 100%.

00:33:54.550 --> 00:33:56.840
So we are running out
of time, unfortunately.

00:33:56.840 --> 00:33:59.050
I mean, we could talk for hours.

00:33:59.050 --> 00:34:00.730
Is there anything we
haven't touched on,

00:34:00.730 --> 00:34:02.104
or something you
want to mention?

00:34:02.104 --> 00:34:03.430
I know Niantic is hiring.

00:34:03.430 --> 00:34:04.390
Do you want to talk about that?

00:34:04.390 --> 00:34:05.860
Like, what can we
wrap up with you?

00:34:05.860 --> 00:34:06.920
EDWARD WU: Yeah, absolutely.

00:34:06.920 --> 00:34:07.670
Niantic is hiring.

00:34:07.670 --> 00:34:12.340
I think we really have a lot
of really great positions open.

00:34:12.340 --> 00:34:16.630
Not only if you have experience
in large scale server or server

00:34:16.630 --> 00:34:20.139
architecture, both
in C# and Java,

00:34:20.139 --> 00:34:23.770
but also on the mobile
clients and device side.

00:34:23.770 --> 00:34:26.320
You asked earlier about what
languages we program in, we

00:34:26.320 --> 00:34:29.434
also program in Unity and C#,
because ultimately we have

00:34:29.434 --> 00:34:31.600
a mobile client that's
beautiful, that's performant.

00:34:31.600 --> 00:34:36.370
And that requires a lot of
not only Unity code, but also

00:34:36.370 --> 00:34:37.889
mobile native code--

00:34:37.889 --> 00:34:40.480
so C++, Java, Objective C--

00:34:40.480 --> 00:34:43.090
in order to make happen.

00:34:43.090 --> 00:34:45.880
I also want to plug one
other bit of where we're

00:34:45.880 --> 00:34:49.150
going as a company, because I
think every part of the company

00:34:49.150 --> 00:34:50.380
will feed into this.

00:34:50.380 --> 00:34:53.170
So, one of the things
that we've invested a lot

00:34:53.170 --> 00:34:55.179
into in the past--

00:34:55.179 --> 00:34:57.580
as part of Pokemon Go,
is to Pokemon Go Plus,

00:34:57.580 --> 00:35:01.390
which is this device that
you can actually wear

00:35:01.390 --> 00:35:04.160
and that will help you play
the game alongside you.

00:35:04.160 --> 00:35:06.380
So that you can actually
have experience playing

00:35:06.380 --> 00:35:08.690
the game that doesn't
involve your mobile phone,

00:35:08.690 --> 00:35:10.167
if you need a hands
off experience

00:35:10.167 --> 00:35:11.750
while you're still
exploring the world

00:35:11.750 --> 00:35:13.910
and playing the
game simultaneously.

00:35:13.910 --> 00:35:16.380
We really think that, even
though the Pokemon Go Plus

00:35:16.380 --> 00:35:21.410
is, right now, just a vibrating
button with some lights,

00:35:21.410 --> 00:35:25.100
that it is a harbinger
of what hardware is going

00:35:25.100 --> 00:35:27.360
to look like in the future.

00:35:27.360 --> 00:35:30.800
And you can see from our very
first concept trailers for this

00:35:30.800 --> 00:35:33.560
game, that we really, really
believe that there's going

00:35:33.560 --> 00:35:36.770
to be-- from all of these
augmented reality hardware

00:35:36.770 --> 00:35:40.130
products that people are
trying out right now,

00:35:40.130 --> 00:35:45.587
things like Microsoft
Hololens, or Google's Tango--

00:35:45.587 --> 00:35:47.420
all these different
technologies that people

00:35:47.420 --> 00:35:48.920
are trying to put out
there to overlay things

00:35:48.920 --> 00:35:50.003
on top of the real world--

00:35:50.003 --> 00:35:51.980
that there will be
mobile technology

00:35:51.980 --> 00:35:56.090
that integrates-- seamlessly
integrates the real world

00:35:56.090 --> 00:35:57.200
with a virtual one.

00:35:57.200 --> 00:36:00.080
And we are very excited to
be at the forefront of that.

00:36:00.080 --> 00:36:04.610
I think we really demonstrated
that augmented reality

00:36:04.610 --> 00:36:08.460
technology is not solely
about the hardware experience.

00:36:08.460 --> 00:36:10.730
Although we are very
excited about integrating

00:36:10.730 --> 00:36:13.040
those hardware experiences
as they come on line.

00:36:13.040 --> 00:36:15.950
But we really believe that
augmented reality is just

00:36:15.950 --> 00:36:19.894
as much about being able to
have a shared state with all

00:36:19.894 --> 00:36:20.810
the people around you.

00:36:20.810 --> 00:36:23.870
So that everybody who
participates in this reality,

00:36:23.870 --> 00:36:27.110
really believes it at
a fundamental level.

00:36:27.110 --> 00:36:29.780
You know, there's something
really magical about the idea

00:36:29.780 --> 00:36:33.530
that when 10 people open
up their mobile phones

00:36:33.530 --> 00:36:37.205
and see a Charizard
further down the block

00:36:37.205 --> 00:36:39.920
together, that they all
orient that in their mind,

00:36:39.920 --> 00:36:41.840
that they all believe
that together.

00:36:41.840 --> 00:36:44.090
And we really believe that
shared social experience

00:36:44.090 --> 00:36:48.410
is actually as much a
part of augmented reality

00:36:48.410 --> 00:36:51.170
as the hardware
experiences will be.

00:36:51.170 --> 00:36:54.830
And at its core, those
social experiences

00:36:54.830 --> 00:36:56.930
are an n squared
problem that can only

00:36:56.930 --> 00:37:00.130
be solved by operating on
a platform of Google Cloud.

00:37:00.130 --> 00:37:01.450
MARK MANDEL: Excellent.

00:37:01.450 --> 00:37:04.255
Well, thank you so much
for joining us today.

00:37:04.255 --> 00:37:05.130
That was fascinating.

00:37:05.130 --> 00:37:07.670
And I think our listeners are
going to really appreciate it.

00:37:07.670 --> 00:37:08.810
EDWARD WU: Thank you very
much for the opportunity.

00:37:08.810 --> 00:37:11.600
I really appreciate the chance
to talk to your listeners,

00:37:11.600 --> 00:37:15.280
and I hope that they get
something out of this as well.

